        <h2>DIY vs Hiring an Agency: The Timeline Trade-Off</h2>
        <p>One of the most common questions we hear is whether to build a website yourself or hire professionals. The timeline difference is dramatic, but so is the outcome difference.</p>

        <p>DIY builders can get you online in days. But the result is a generic site that looks like thousands of others, converts poorly, and limits your growth. You will also spend 20–40 hours of your own time learning the platform, troubleshooting issues, and making design decisions without strategic guidance.</p>

        <p>Hiring an agency takes longer — typically 4–8 weeks — but the result is a custom-built asset designed specifically for your business. You get strategic guidance, professional design, clean code, and ongoing support. Most importantly, you get a site that actually generates leads and revenue.</p>

        <p>For a deeper comparison, read our full guide on <a href="/blog/diy-website-vs-hiring-an-agency/">DIY website vs hiring an agency</a>. For a detailed cost breakdown, see <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-website-cost/">how much does a website cost in 2026</a>.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>How long does it take to build a simple 5-page website?</h3>
        <p>A simple 5-page brochure website takes 3–5 weeks with a professional agency. This includes discovery, design, development, and testing. With a freelancer using a template, it can take 2–4 weeks. With a DIY builder, it can be done in 1–3 days, though the quality and customization will be limited.</p>

        <h3>How long does it take to build an e-commerce website?</h3>
        <p>An e-commerce website typically takes 8–16 weeks. The timeline depends on the number of products, payment integrations, shipping configurations, and any custom features. A small store with 10–20 products on Shopify can be launched in 4–6 weeks. A custom-built e-commerce platform with hundreds of products and complex functionality can take 3–6 months.</p>

        <h3>What is the fastest way to build a website?</h3>
        <p>The fastest way is a DIY website builder like Wix or Squarespace. You can launch a basic site in a few hours. However, speed comes at the cost of customization, performance, and conversion potential. For businesses that need to generate revenue, a professional build is faster in the long run because it does not require a rebuild 6–12 months later.</p>

        <h3>Can a website be built in one week?</h3>
        <p>Technically, yes — if you use a template or DIY builder and have all content ready. However, a one-week build skips discovery, strategy, and quality assurance. The result is usually a site that looks rushed, performs poorly, and fails to convert. For any business that depends on its website for leads or sales, one week is not enough.</p>

        <h3>What slows down a website project the most?</h3>
        <p>Late content delivery is the single biggest cause of delays. Other major factors include slow client feedback, scope changes mid-project, too many stakeholders, and unprepared brand assets. The good news is that all of these are preventable with proper planning and clear communication.</p>

        <h3>How can I speed up my website build?</h3>
        <p>Prepare your content before the project starts. Designate one decision-maker for feedback. Lock the project scope during discovery. Gather all brand assets upfront. Respond to feedback requests within 48 hours. And choose an agency with a proven process — like <a href="/process/">ours at OnwardCraft</a> — that is designed to keep projects on track.</p>

        <p class="conclusion">The timeline for building a website in 2026 ranges from a few hours to several months. The right timeline for your project depends on your goals, your budget, and how you want your site to perform. A rushed build might get you online faster, but a disciplined, professional build will get you results that last. If you are ready to build a website that drives real business growth, <a href="/book-call/" rel="noopener noreferrer">let us talk</a>.</p>

